#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130369 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ First, we create a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe do not consider negative values for the input because the square root of a negative number is not a real number.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/39/39c60b7e997c4af7b823796c0b94144572e7bff1be083564ab9fa29fd66d4a42.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- The domain  is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The range  is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large." :tutorial/id #uuid "ba6d6815-5a19-50e1-ae6a-8182c47cdba8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3789 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
